<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Modhera Sun Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1026–1027</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sun Temple at Modhera in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to Surya, built in 1026–1027 under the Chaulukya king Bhima I. It consists of a sanctum, an assembly hall and a large stepped tank, the Surya Kund, all richly carved, and it is aligned so that the rising sun lights the sanctum at the equinoxes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/modhera-sun-temple.jpg" alt="Modhera Sun Temple" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Musafir kanya · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surya_mandhir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
